<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rani Mangammal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1705</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rani Mangammal was queen regent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> kingdom from about 1689 to 1704, on behalf of her grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijaya-ranga-chokkanatha">Vijaya Ranga Chokkanatha</a>. She kept the kingdom intact through diplomacy with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> and Travancore. She is remembered for building roads, rest houses, water tanks and choultries across the southern Tamil country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rani-mangammal.jpg" alt="Rani Mangammal" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suresh, Madurai · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tirumalai_Nayak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mangammal, also called Mangamma, was the daughter of Tupakula Lingama Nayaka. When her husband died in 1682 he was succeeded by her son, Rangakrishna Muthu Virappa Nayak, who ruled until his death in 1689. Her son's widow was then pregnant, and the throne passed to Mangammal's grandson. The young king's mother committed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a>, and Mangammal became regent during his minority. The capital of the kingdom in her time was Tiruchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Her regency is remembered for its public works, many of them still in use. Irrigation channels were repaired, new roads built and avenue trees planted, and temples and municipal buildings were completed, among them her Spring Palace at TumKum, which now houses the Gandhi Memorial Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>. The highway from Cape Comorin, first built in her time, was known as Rani Mangammal Salai. She also first held the Unjal festival in the Meenakshi temple in the Tamil month of Ani, when the royal families visit the temple and pay tribute to Meenakshi Amman.</p><p>She aided the Mughal army at the Siege of Jinji. Seeing that Rajaram, entrenched in Jinji, meant to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> if the Mughal army withdrew, she recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> as her suzerain and helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfiqar-khan">Zulfiqar Khan</a> against the fort. When it was taken after eight years, she and her family held it under Mughal leadership.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When her grandson came of age in 1704, Mangammal and her prime minister, Achayya, refused to give up power. They were seized by the army commander and executed. She is still widely remembered as a maker of roads and avenues and a builder of temples, tanks and choultries, and for her diplomatic and political skill. A portrait of her made in her own time can still be seen in the Unjal Mandapam of the Meenakshi temple.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mangammal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Queen <b>Mangammal</b>, regent around 1700, built roads, rest houses and tanks.</blockquote><a href="/state/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Madurai Nayak dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.92,78.12&amp;year=1695" aria-label="Madurai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6704 1793 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rani Mangammal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7154 2063h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7154 2063h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9.92,78.12&amp;year=1695">What was here in 1695? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
